## 3. Honesty discipline

These ship seeded because each is a measured failure class rather than a taste.
They are all one idea in different costumes: **a surface must not be able to
report something it did not establish.**

**3.1 A status or diagnostic never returns green on an empty, failed or
malformed read.** Three states, never two: **ok**, **problem**, **unknown**. A
read that throws, times out, returns nothing, or returns something unparseable
yields `unknown` and renders distinctly. Wrapping a read in a broad handler that
returns an empty collection converts "could not look" into "looked, found
nothing wrong" - and the failure becomes invisible exactly when it matters.
*Check:* make the source unreadable in a test and assert the surface does not
read green.

**3.2 Honest absence, measured zero and failure are three different things.**
"No records exist", "the measurement ran and the answer is 0", and "the
measurement did not run" must be distinguishable in the data, not only in a log
line. A renderer that maps all three to `0` is lying in two of the three cases.
Carry the distinction in the type - an optional value, a status field, a
sentinel that is not a number - and never let a default fill it in silently.

**3.3 A fallback whose output can equal its input is not a fallback, for
anything that produces variety.** If a generator of distinct values - names,
keys, ids, sampled variants, colours, orderings - can fall back to a constant,
the feature has two indistinguishable states: working, and not running at all.
Either raise, or return something **visibly marked** as a fallback and record it
as such. *Check:* empty the generator's source and assert it refuses or marks;
then assert two successive calls against a populated source differ.

**3.4 Two verbs that reach the same end state share the same preconditions.**
If `archive()` and `close()` both end with the record closed, they validate the
same things, emit the same records, and refuse in the same cases. A second path
to a state is where the guard gets forgotten - one route checks permission and
the other does not, and the difference is invisible until somebody takes the
other route. *Check:* when you add a path to an existing state, list every
existing path to it and diff their preconditions line by line.

**3.5 Platform-varying spellings are derived in tests, never hardcoded.**
Exception class names, path separators, line endings, locale-formatted numbers,
temp directory layouts and error message text vary by platform and by version. A
test asserting a literal spelling passes where it was written and fails
somewhere else, which reads as a real defect and costs somebody an afternoon.
Derive the expected value from the same source the code uses - catch the
exception type rather than matching its name, join paths rather than writing a
separator, compare parsed values rather than formatted strings.

**3.6 Persisted positions use durable keys, never bare ordinals.** State that
points into an ordered collection stores the identity of the thing - a name, a
slug, a stable id - not index 7. An ordinal is meaningful only relative to a
list that will be reordered, and when it is, nothing errors: the system simply
resumes somewhere else. Where an ordinal must be kept for compatibility, version
the state format and resolve key-first with the ordinal as a clamped fallback.
*Check:* reorder the collection in a test and assert restored state still points
at the same item.

**3.7 A swallowed exception is judged by what the CALLER now believes.** The
question is never "is this handler reasonable here"; it is "what does the code
one level up conclude from the value this handler returned". A handler that
returns an empty list makes the caller print "no problems"; one that returns the
previous value makes the caller render stale data as current. Decide the
caller's belief first, then write the handler that produces it. *Check:* for
every broad `except`/`catch` in the diff, write the sentence the caller will now
say out loud. If that sentence is not true, the handler is the defect.

**3.8 A boolean named for an observation must not be consumed as a
conclusion.** `saw_no_errors`, `file_missing`, `queue_empty` record what was
observed; `is_healthy`, `never_ran`, `work_is_done` assert what is true. One is
derived from a look that may not have happened. When an observation flag is read
at a call site as though it settled the question, the failure is silent and
total. *Check:* grep the flag's call sites. Any use where a failed or skipped
observation and a genuine negative produce the same branch is this class; split
into three states (section 3.1) or rename the flag to what it actually saw.

**3.9 An empty lookup is evidence of absence only if the query could have
HIT.** A search against the wrong namespace, an unmigrated table, a path that
does not exist, or an identifier scheme the store never used returns zero rows
and looks exactly like "there is nothing there". Absence is only meaningful
against a query proven capable of a positive. *Check:* every "nothing found"
branch is paired with a positive control - a test that the same query shape
returns a known row from the same source. Without the control, report unknown.

**3.10 A probe that cannot reach its subject returns UNKNOWN, never a claim
about the subject.** Unreachable, timed out, not installed, no permission: each
is a fact about the probe, not about the thing probed. Reporting "not running"
because the check could not connect converts a broken instrument into a
diagnosis. **The mirror is equally binding:** an empty answer from a source that
WAS reached is a real answer and must not be downgraded to unknown, or every
true negative disappears into noise. *Check:* two tests per probe - subject
unreachable yields unknown; subject reachable and genuinely empty yields the
empty answer, distinctly.

**3.11 A liveness or timeout budget is a measurement, and its timeout branch
must not equal its absence branch.** A budget is set from the measured worst
observed response plus headroom - never from a round number that felt generous,
and never from the happy-path average. Cold start, first-load and
contended-resource paths are part of the measurement. Separately: "the answer
did not arrive in time" and "there is no answer" must land in different
branches, because collapsing them turns a slow dependency into a confidently
reported absence. *Check:* record the measured worst case beside the constant,
and assert in a test that a timeout and a genuine empty produce different
outcomes.

**3.12 One root of trust per resource; a sentinel that means two things gets
read as the safer one.** When two files, two flags or two fields can each
answer "is this enabled / present / current", they will disagree, and the reader
picks whichever it checked first. Likewise a single sentinel value pressed into
two meanings - `0` for "off" and for "unset", `null` for "absent" and for "not
yet computed" - is resolved by every reader in the direction that lets work
continue. *Check:* name the one authoritative source in the code and make every
other surface derive from it; give each meaning its own representable value.

**3.13 Any sentence of the form "run this on the target machine" must name the
automation channel that cannot carry it, or it is a defect in the plan.** A
crew with access to a machine automates the work AND ships the result back; a
plan that hands the owner a command has either found a genuine gap - which is
then stated, with the channel that is missing - or has skipped the design step.
The owner is asked to LOOK at a result, not to produce one. *Check:* grep plans,
runbooks and guard messages for "please run", "on the box", "you will need to",
and for each hit either name the channel and the reason it cannot carry the
work, or automate it.

**3.14 A one-time or migration script exits non-zero on every path where the
work did not complete.** Runners trust the exit code and stamp a success marker
from it. A script that returns 0 from a dependency-absent skip, a
nothing-to-do-yet branch, or a caught-and-logged failure is recorded as applied
on every machine and never runs again - so the fix ships nowhere and the
registry says it shipped everywhere. *Check:* enumerate the script's exit paths
and assert the code for each; verify idempotency explicitly before trusting a
re-run, because "safe to re-run" is a property nobody tested.

**3.15 Bind every test double to the live signature of what it stands in for.**
A fake written from the call site you just typed, rather than from the class it
replaces, passes every test and every mutation, then raises on its first real
call. Mutation testing cannot catch an invented API, because both the code and
the double are wrong in the same direction. *Check:* build doubles against the
real signature and the real field set, taken from the production definition at
test time. The one-line smell test: a keyword argument that appears only at its
own call site was invented.

**3.16 A fixture that builds its own schema is a second implementation.** A
hand-rolled record shape inside a test drifts toward whatever the test needs,
and then the suite is green against a structure production does not produce.
Import the production definition and construct from it. *Check:* no test
declares the fields of a type the code under test owns.

**3.17 A mutation anchor is unique, and the assertion it kills has nothing else
to be true about.** An anchor matched in two places may edit the wrong one, and
an anchor matched nowhere changes nothing while reading exactly like "the code
is fine". Separately, an assertion satisfied by a longer name containing the
short one, by an `or` branch, or by a second copy of the same string elsewhere
in the output survives deletion of the thing it is named after. *Check:* assert
the anchor occurs exactly once (count, not membership), and before accepting a
red, ask what ELSE could make this assertion true and remove that too.

**3.18 Test a guard through the seam that actually runs.** A test that calls the
helper directly proves the helper works; it proves nothing about the caller that
was supposed to consult it, and the common defect is a guard that is correct and
unwired. *Check:* the regression test enters through the public path the
incident came in on, with the guard's inputs arranged, not through the private
function the fix happened to touch.

**3.19 <PROJECT_SPECIFIC_HONESTY_RULE>.** <Add yours here as they are learned.
Every entry names the check that catches the next one.>

