## 4.5 Root cause, not repair

Numbered 4.5 deliberately: it slots between the bug-class list and the style
rules without renumbering anything that other documents cite.

A repair makes this instance go away. A root cause explains why the instance
was possible, and its fix removes the possibility. Five rules make the
difference checkable rather than a matter of how thorough somebody felt.

- **The whys are CHAINED.** Each level answers the previous level's *answer*,
  not the original symptom. Five restatements of one observation is not an
  analysis; it is the same sentence five times in different words.
- **A level that names a person, a moment, or an amount of attention is not a
  cause.** "It was missed in review", "the author was rushing", "nobody
  noticed" are all the same non-cause: they describe who was standing there.
  Keep going until the level names a property of the system - a missing gate, a
  representable-but-invalid state, an interface that permits the wrong call.
- **Start the chain at the earliest observable symptom**, not where it was
  noticed. The place a failure surfaced is usually the last honest component in
  the chain, and analysing from there produces a fix in the wrong module.
- **Detection is luck and is listed under prevention, never under cause.** "It
  was caught by an unrelated test" is a statement about fortune. The prevention
  section says what would have caught it *deliberately*, and whether that check
  now exists.
- **An accepted limitation names WHO owns closing it and WHAT would close
  it.** Without both, "accepted" is deferral wearing acceptance's name, and it
  will be rediscovered as a new defect by somebody who was not in the room.

*Check:* read the chain bottom-up. If the last level does not name something
that could be changed in the codebase, the analysis stopped early.

---

## 4.6 Verification fires on events, not on doubt

The cheapest checks are the most reliably skipped, because "I am fairly sure"
is free and running the check is not. Doubt is the wrong trigger: it is highest
where the work is unfamiliar and lowest where the work is routine, which is
exactly backwards from where defects land. So each check is bound to a
mechanical event in the work itself.

| When this happens | This check runs, without being asked |
|---|---|
| You wrote a rule, guard or policy | Run it over the **whole existing population**, not the one case that prompted it. A rule is a claim about every member. |
| You wrote a matcher, parser or classifier | Drive it with **one must-reject and one must-accept**. A matcher tested only on the case it was written for is a restatement of that case. |
| You wrote a comment claiming a guarantee | Land the guarantee in the **same edit** - the assertion, the type, the test. A comment is not an enforcement mechanism. |
| A fix failed twice | Re-diagnose the **model**, not the patch. Two failed attempts means the explanation is wrong; a third patch against the same explanation is a coin toss. |
| A reviewer says something is already fixed | **Verify that claim too.** It has the same standing as any other unverified finding, and it arrives when you most want it to be true. |
| You changed a derived or generated artifact by hand | Regenerate it with its tool and diff. A hand-edit that happens to be correct today still removes the generator as the source of truth. |

*Check:* each row above is falsifiable in review - the reviewer can ask for the
population run, the two matcher cases, or the re-diagnosis, and the absence of
one is a finding rather than a preference.

